{
  "assessments": [
    {
      "claim": "H7",
      "status": "accept",
      "investigation": "closed",
      "evidence": [
        "R7",
        "R26"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "narrative": "The independent validation result for H7 is strongly supportive. Under the registered direction -1, validation R26 gives a signed estimate of +0.8006 with an independent interval entirely positive, [0.7713, 0.8300], at alpha=0.003846. Because the estimate is already signed to the hypothesis direction, the raw SMARCA4-loss minus SMARCA4-intact difference in dependency_SMARCA2 is approximately -0.80, meaning SMARCA4-loss cells have substantially more negative dependency_SMARCA2 scores, i.e., stronger SMARCA2 dependency. Discovery R7 was consistent: signed estimate +0.7840, interval [0.7646, 0.8034]. The validation estimate is nearly identical to discovery and the independent interval excludes zero even under a stringent multiplicity-adjusted threshold. The magnitude is very large: it is more than two outcome SDs and far exceeds the 10% meaningful-difference threshold, although relative-difference framing is awkward because dependency scores are centered near zero. I therefore accept H7 as a robust, independently validated dataset-level association: SMARCA4 loss is associated with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency. I close H7 as the primary claim because the direction and magnitude are now well characterized in this dataset. This association is biologically plausible as a synthetic-lethality-like relationship, but the data remain associational; they do not establish causality or generalizability beyond this dataset. The most informative next analysis is independent validation of H9, MSI-high versus WRN dependency, which is the next accepted but not yet independently validated large effect. Negative findings from prior robustness tests remain meaningful: the original H5 KEAP1/NFE2L2 hypothesis is rejected and reversed H19 is validated; marker-D negative controls and marker-D interactions for SMARCA2, WRN, and NFE2L2 are null/close-to-null; and the WRN subtype interaction is null. Conditional accepted subgroup effects for SMARCA2 and WRN remain consistent but are not independently validated and should be interpreted only as dataset-specific subgroup associations. Many driver-gene hypotheses remain unresolved or unresolved near-null and should not be presented as supported."
}
